From: OpenWrt packaging fix
Subject: fastddc.c: make is_integer() static inline

is_integer() is declared as plain C99 "inline" (no "static", no
matching extern declaration anywhere - fastddc.h does not declare
it). Under strict ISO C99 inline semantics, a plain "inline"
definition is NOT guaranteed to emit a linkable out-of-line body at
all: whether that's needed depends entirely on whether the compiler
happens to inline every call site.

At upstream's own -O3 (and at plain -O2) GCC inlines the single call
site in fastddc_init() and everything links. At -Os - what OpenWrt's
TARGET_CFLAGS actually requests for space-constrained targets, and
what ends up as the *effective* optimization level here since it's
appended after csdr's own -O2 on the command line - GCC declines to
inline it and, per the standard, is not obligated to leave a callable
copy behind either. Result:

  ld: ./libcsdr.so: undefined reference to `is_integer'

"static inline" has well-defined behavior in every C mode (the
compiler is always free to inline it, or always free to emit a
private, translation-unit-local copy) and removes the dependency on
inlining heuristics entirely. is_integer() is only ever called from
within fastddc.c itself, so static is exactly the linkage it needs -
no other file references it (confirmed: is_nan() and
fir_one_pass_ff(), which face the same plain-inline pattern, are each
also declared as an ordinary prototype in libcsdr.h, which is what
correctly gives *them* external linkage already; is_integer has no
such declaration anywhere, which is what makes it the one broken
case).

--- a/fastddc.c
+++ b/fastddc.c
@@ -33,7 +33,7 @@ SOFTWARE, EVEN IF ADVISED OF THE POSSIBILITY OF SUCH DAMAGE.
 //DDC implementation based on:
 //http://www.3db-labs.com/01598092_MultibandFilterbank.pdf
 
-inline int is_integer(float a) { return floorf(a) == a; }
+static inline int is_integer(float a) { return floorf(a) == a; }
 
 int fastddc_init(fastddc_t* ddc, float transition_bw, int decimation, float shift_rate)
 {
